## Quick‑Reference Cheat‑Sheet

| # | Common Pitfall | Why it hurts | Quick Fix / Best Practice |
|---|----------------|--------------|---------------------------|
| 1 | **Assuming the code runs immediately** | `yield return` creates *deferred execution*. Nothing happens until the sequence is enumerated. | Materialize early (`ToList()`, `ToArray()`) when you need the data up front. |
| 2 | **Modifying the source collection while iterating** | The enumerator keeps a version stamp; changing the collection throws `InvalidOperationException`. | Copy the collection first (`var copy = source.ToList()`), or use a thread‑safe collection. |
| 3 | **Capturing loop variables in closures** | A lambda inside a `yield return` captures the *loop variable*, not the value. All yields may return the same last value. | Use a local variable inside the loop (`var local = i; yield return local;`) or a `for (int i = 0; ...)` with a separate temp. |
| 4 | **Using `return` after `yield return` in the same block** | `return` inside an iterator method is only allowed *at the end* of the method; mixing it with `yield return` in the same block is confusing and can lead to unreachable code. | Let the method fall out of the loop or use `yield break` to terminate early. |
| 5 | **Enumerating a sequence that contains `null` or incomplete data** | Returning `null` in an `IEnumerable<T>` can cause `NullReferenceException` on the consumer. | Validate data before yielding, or throw a more descriptive exception. |
| 6 | **Combining `yield return` with `async`/`await`** | `yield return` cannot coexist with `await`. Trying to do so causes a compile‑time error. | Use `IAsyncEnumerable<T>` and `await foreach` for async streams. |
| 7 | **Not disposing resources inside the iterator** | A `yield return` block automatically implements `IDisposable`, but if you open a stream or lock inside it, you must clean up manually. | Wrap resource usage in `try/finally` inside the iterator:  

  ```csharp
  public IEnumerable<string> ReadLines(string path)
  {
      using var sr = new StreamReader(path);
      string line;
      while ((line = sr.ReadLine()) != null)
          yield return line;
  }
  ``` |
| 8 | **Using `yield return` in a constructor, property setter, or static constructor** | These contexts cannot contain an iterator block. | Move the iterator to a normal method. |
| 9 | **Returning `void` or non‑`IEnumerable` type** | Compiler error; iterator methods must return `IEnumerable<T>`, `IEnumerable`, `IEnumerator<T>`, or `IEnumerator`. | Change the return type accordingly. |
| 10 | **Using `yield return` for trivial loops** | The state machine generated by the compiler adds overhead. | Prefer a simple `for`/`foreach` if you’re not doing lazy evaluation. |
| 11 | **Not handling exceptions thrown by the enumerator** | Exceptions are deferred until enumeration; if you swallow them incorrectly, debugging is hard. | Let exceptions propagate or catch them inside the iterator if you need to translate or log. |
| 12 | **Creating infinite sequences and enumerating forever** | You may accidentally exhaust memory or lock the UI thread. | Provide a clear stop condition or expose `IAsyncEnumerable` with cancellation. |
| 13 | **Using `yield break` incorrectly inside a `try` block** | The `try` block will still execute the `finally` even if you `yield break`. | Structure your `try/finally` carefully; `yield break` is fine, but remember it ends the iteration immediately. |
| 14 | **Assuming `foreach` will enumerate all items immediately** | The consumer can stop early, leaving resources un‑released. | Use `finally` in the consumer to clean up or use `using` with `IAsyncEnumerable`. |
| 15 | **Failing to account for thread safety** | Enumerators are not thread‑safe; concurrent reads can cause race conditions. | Ensure the underlying collection is immutable or use synchronization primitives. |

